   "head": "Course Outline\nACCTG 1A - Financial Accounting\nUnits: 0.00\nLecture Units: 0.00\nLecture Hours: Standard: 0.00 Range: 0.00 - 0.00\nOutside-of-Class Hours: Standard: 0.00 Range: 0.00 - 0.00\nTotal Student Learning Hours: Standard: 0.00 Range: 0.00 - 0.00\nGrading Methods: Letter Grade\nDistance Education: None\n\nCredit for Prior Learning\nCredit for Prior Learning No\nConditions of Enrollment\nAdvisory: CIS 1; BUSTEC 63.\nCourse Description\nDevelopment and communication of financial information that is useful for decision-making. Course material covered includes the accounting environment; basic financial statements; accounting cycle; and operating, investing, and financing activities.\nCourse Objectives\nPrepare and analyze operating, investing, and financing activities of the cash flow statement.\nAnalyze internal control problems to ensure the integrity of financial information.\nUse accounting information in business decisions.\nDemonstrate accounting computer skills.\nExplain accounting and its relationship to society.\nDemonstrate the ability to communicate through written, oral, and class projects.\nUnderstand the role of ethics in accounting.\nComplete an accounting project using a general ledger software package (Quick Books or Carol Yacht). The project includes identifying transactions, recording transactions and preparing the four GAAP required financial statements (Income Statement, Statement of Retained Earnings, Balance Sheet, and Cash Flow Statement).\nCourse Content/Multiple Means of Engagement and Representation (UDL)\nUniversal Design for Learning (UDL) - Multiple Means of Engagement and Representation: Present content in varied formats like text, audio, video, visuals, and interactive elements while also offering students meaningful options for choice, collaboration, relevance, and motivation, so learners can access information in ways that work for them and stay interested, challenged, and actively involved in their learning.\n\nDEIA Opportunity: Incorporate diverse contributions to the discipline, use inclusive language and/or identify opportunities for students to connect content to their sociocultural backgrounds.\nContent\n\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0Introducing Accounting in Business\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Ethical decision-making models within Accounting\n\nAnalyzing and Recording Transactions\nAdjusting Accounts and Preparing Financial Statements\nAccounting for Merchandising Operations\nInventories and Cost of Sales\nCash and Internal Controls\nAccounts and Notes Receivables\nLong-Term Assets\nCurrent Lia",
